(window.__PQ=window.__PQ||{})["03-docker"]={"slug":"03-docker","title":"Docker and Containers","accent":"#60a5fa","sections":[{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms","title":"Containers versus virtual machines","c":[{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms-c1","t":"What a container really is","p":"A container is a normal process (or group of processes) started with a restricted view of the machine: its own process list, network, hostname and filesystem. It shares the host kernel with every other container.","k":[["Do containers have their own kernel?","No. They share the host kernel; only the view of processes, network and files is separate."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms-c2","t":"Containers versus virtual machines","p":"A virtual machine emulates hardware and runs a complete guest operating system with its own kernel. A container skips all that and uses the host kernel directly.","k":[],"o":[["Container","packaging and running your own trusted services","running untrusted code from unknown tenants on a shared kernel"],["Virtual machine","different operating systems or strict tenant isolation","you need thousands of tiny, fast-starting units"],["MicroVM","multi-tenant platforms such as serverless","you need to run arbitrary host devices or GPUs directly"]]},{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms-c3","t":"Docker architecture","p":"Docker is several parts. The docker command is a client that talks over a socket to the Docker daemon (dockerd). The daemon builds images and delegates running containers to containerd, which uses runc to create them.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms-c4","t":"Container lifecycle","p":"A container moves through states: created, running, paused, exited, removed. Its writable data lives only as long as the container exists unless you store it in a volume.","k":[["Why does docker stop sometimes take 10 seconds?","The app ignored SIGTERM (often because the shell is PID 1), so Docker waited the grace period and sent SIGKILL."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms-c5","t":"Running options you will use daily","p":"docker run has dozens of flags. A small set covers nearly everything: ports, environment, volumes, resource limits and the user.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-containers-vs-vms-c6","t":"Containers on AWS and Kubernetes","p":"The same container image runs on your laptop, in CI, on ECS or Fargate and in a Kubernetes pod. What differs is who starts it and how.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage","title":"Images and layers","c":[{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage-c1","t":"What an image is","p":"An image is a read-only bundle: a filesystem split into layers, plus a small JSON config that records the default command, environment variables, user and exposed ports.","k":[["What stores the default command of an image?","The image config JSON (Cmd and Entrypoint), referenced by the manifest."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage-c2","t":"Copy on write and the union filesystem","p":"Layers are stacked so they look like one directory tree. When a container changes a file from a lower layer, the file is first copied up to the writable layer, then changed there.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage-c3","t":"Content addressable storage","p":"Everything in an image is named by the hash of its content. Change one byte and the name changes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage-c4","t":"Image size and why it matters","p":"Size drives pull time, cold start on a new Kubernetes node, registry cost and attack surface. A 1.2 GB image takes much longer to start on a fresh node than a 60 MB one.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage-c5","t":"Tags, names and references","p":"An image reference has the form registry/namespace/repository:tag@digest. Missing parts get defaults: Docker Hub, the library namespace and the latest tag.","k":[["Why is latest a poor production tag?","It moves whenever someone pushes, so two deployments of the same name may run different code and rollbacks are not reproducible."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-images-layers-storage-c6","t":"Saving, loading and cleaning images","p":"Images can be moved without a registry as a tar file, and disk can fill quickly with unused layers and build cache.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups","title":"Namespaces, cgroups and container isolation","c":[{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups-c1","t":"Namespaces","p":"A namespace wraps a global system resource so a process sees its own private copy. Processes in the same namespace see each other; others do not.","k":[["Which namespace lets two containers both listen on port 8080?","The network namespace, because each has its own port space."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups-c2","t":"Building a container by hand","p":"You can create a mini container with one command: unshare starts a program inside new namespaces. A container runtime does this plus a root filesystem and cgroups.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups-c3","t":"Control groups (cgroups)","p":"A cgroup is a kernel group of processes with limits and accounting. Namespaces control what a process can see; cgroups control how much it can use.","k":[["What do the two numbers in cpu.max 50000 100000 mean?","A quota of 50 ms of CPU time per 100 ms period, which equals 0.5 CPU."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups-c4","t":"Memory limits and the OOM killer","p":"When a container uses more memory than its limit, the kernel kills a process in it. The exit code is 137 and the container is marked OOMKilled.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups-c5","t":"CPU limits and throttling","p":"CPU limits do not kill; they pause. A container with --cpus 0.5 may run only 50 ms in each 100 ms window, then waits.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-namespaces-cgroups-c6","t":"Linux features that harden containers","p":"Beyond namespaces and cgroups, three features cut what a contained process can do: capabilities, seccomp and LSM profiles (AppArmor or SELinux).","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics","title":"Dockerfile fundamentals","c":[{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics-c1","t":"Anatomy of a Dockerfile","p":"A Dockerfile is a text file of instructions run top to bottom by the builder. The first must be FROM, which names the starting image.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics-c2","t":"COPY, ADD and the build context","p":"docker build . sends the directory . (the context) to the builder. COPY can only read files from inside it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics-c3","t":"RUN, shell form and exec form","p":"RUN runs commands while building. Instructions come in two syntaxes: shell form (RUN apt-get update) runs via /bin/sh -c; exec form ([\"cmd\",\"arg\"]) runs the program directly.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics-c4","t":"CMD versus ENTRYPOINT","p":"ENTRYPOINT is the fixed program; CMD is the default arguments, easy to override at docker run.","k":[["What is the Kubernetes equivalent of ENTRYPOINT?","The container field command; args maps to CMD."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics-c5","t":"Exec form and signal handling","p":"Shell form wraps your program in /bin/sh -c, making the shell PID 1. The shell does not forward SIGTERM, so the app never shuts down gracefully.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-dockerfile-basics-c6","t":"ENV, ARG and configuration","p":"ARG exists only during build. ENV persists into the image and every running container.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s05-caching-multistage","title":"Build cache and multi-stage builds","c":[{"id":"s05-caching-multistage-c1","t":"How the layer cache works","p":"For each instruction BuildKit checks whether it already built the same instruction on the same parent layer with the same inputs. If yes, it reuses the result; if not, that step and every step after it run again.","k":[["Why copy requirements.txt before the rest of the code?","Its checksum rarely changes, so the pip install layer stays cached when only source files change."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-caching-multistage-c2","t":"Multi-stage builds","p":"A Dockerfile can have several FROM lines. Each starts a new stage. The final image contains only the last stage, and you copy chosen files into it from earlier stages.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-caching-multistage-c3","t":"Python multi-stage with a virtualenv","p":"Python packages with native extensions need compilers to install. Build them in one stage into a virtualenv, then copy only that folder into a clean runtime stage.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-caching-multistage-c4","t":"BuildKit cache mounts","p":"RUN --mount=type=cache gives a build step a directory that persists between builds but is never stored in an image layer.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-caching-multistage-c5","t":"Build secrets and SSH mounts","p":"RUN --mount=type=secret makes a file available only during one build step. It is not written into any layer or the image history.","k":[["Where can you read a secret mounted with --mount=type=secret?","Only inside the RUN step that mounts it, at /run/secrets/<id> by default."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-caching-multistage-c6","t":"Heredocs, syntax directive and linting","p":"Modern Dockerfile syntax allows multi-line scripts with heredocs and a version directive that enables newer features without upgrading Docker.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile","title":"Production ready Dockerfiles","c":[{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile-c1","t":"Running as non-root","p":"By default the process in a container is root (UID 0). Create an unprivileged user and switch to it with USER.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile-c2","t":"HEALTHCHECK","p":"HEALTHCHECK tells Docker how to test whether the app inside works, not merely whether the process is alive.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile-c3","t":"Graceful shutdown","p":"When asked to stop, your application should finish current requests, close connections and exit before the grace period ends.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile-c4","t":"Labels, pinning and reproducibility","p":"Labels are metadata on the image. Pinned bases and lock files make the same source produce the same image.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile-c5","t":"A complete Python image","p":"Putting the pieces together: multi-stage build, venv, non-root, health check, exec-form command and OCI labels.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-production-dockerfile-c6","t":"Signals, PID 1 and the init process","p":"The first process in a container is PID 1. The kernel treats it specially: signals without a handler are ignored, and orphaned child processes are re-parented to it.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts","title":"Volumes, bind mounts and tmpfs","c":[{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts-c1","t":"Why containers lose data","p":"The writable layer belongs to one container. Remove the container and the layer is deleted with it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts-c2","t":"Named volumes","p":"A named volume is storage managed by Docker. You refer to it by name and Docker decides where it lives on the host.","k":[["Does docker rm delete named volumes?","No. Only anonymous volumes with rm -v, or an explicit docker volume rm, remove them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts-c3","t":"Bind mounts","p":"A bind mount maps an exact host file or directory into the container. Both sides see the same files immediately.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts-c4","t":"tmpfs and read-only roots","p":"A tmpfs mount lives in RAM. Combined with a read-only root filesystem, it gives an app a writable scratch area while the image stays untouchable.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts-c5","t":"Permissions and ownership","p":"Files in a volume carry numeric user IDs. If the container runs as UID 10001 but the files belong to root, writes fail with permission denied.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-volumes-bind-mounts-c6","t":"Backup, restore and Kubernetes mapping","p":"A volume is only a directory, so you can archive it with a throwaway container that mounts it.","k":[["Which Kubernetes object replaces a Docker named volume for a database?","A PersistentVolumeClaim, usually through a StatefulSet volumeClaimTemplate."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host","title":"Container networking basics","c":[{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host-c1","t":"Network drivers","p":"A Docker network is a driver plus settings. The common drivers are bridge (single host), host, none, overlay (multi host) and macvlan.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host-c2","t":"The default bridge and veth pairs","p":"Docker creates a Linux bridge (docker0). Each container gets a virtual cable (veth pair): one end inside as eth0, the other plugged into the bridge.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host-c3","t":"Publishing ports","p":"-p 8080:80 asks Docker to forward host port 8080 to container port 80. Without it nothing outside the host can reach the container.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host-c4","t":"User defined networks and DNS","p":"On a network you create yourself, Docker runs an embedded DNS server at 127.0.0.11. Containers resolve each other by container name or service alias.","k":[["Why can containers on the default bridge not resolve each other by name?","Embedded DNS is only enabled on user-defined networks; the legacy bridge needs links or IPs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host-c5","t":"Host, overlay and macvlan in practice","p":"Special drivers trade isolation for performance or reach. Use them deliberately.","k":[],"o":[["bridge","almost every application","you need raw LAN presence"],["host","agents, sniffers, latency critical proxies","you run several copies of the same port"],["overlay","Swarm services across hosts","you run Kubernetes, use its CNI"],["macvlan","legacy apps needing a LAN IP","the switch limits MAC addresses per port"]]},{"id":"s08-networking-bridge-host-c6","t":"Troubleshooting container networking","p":"When a container cannot reach something, test each hop in order: its own interface, the bridge, DNS, the destination port and finally the host firewall.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals","title":"Docker Compose fundamentals","c":[{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals-c1","t":"What Compose is","p":"Compose reads a YAML file (compose.yaml) that lists services, networks and volumes, and calls the Docker engine to create them. A set of running services is called a project.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals-c2","t":"A complete Python stack","p":"A realistic file: a FastAPI app built from a local Dockerfile, a Postgres database with a persistent volume, and Redis.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals-c3","t":"Networks, DNS and ports in Compose","p":"Compose creates one network called <project>default and attaches every service. Services find each other by service name.","k":[["Why does connecting to localhost:5432 from the API container fail?","Localhost is the API container itself; the database is reached at db:5432."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals-c4","t":"Volumes and bind mounts in Compose","p":"Compose declares named volumes at the top level and mounts them into services. A relative path such as ./src becomes a bind mount.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals-c5","t":"Environment variables and .env files","p":"Compose can substitute variables into the YAML itself and pass them into containers. These are two different things.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-compose-fundamentals-c6","t":"Build, scale and override files","p":"Compose merges several files, so one base file serves development, test and CI with small overrides.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s10-compose-advanced","title":"Compose profiles, health and secrets","c":[{"id":"s10-compose-advanced-c1","t":"depends_on conditions","p":"dependson controls start order. By default it waits only until the dependency container has started, not until it is ready to serve.","k":[["What does service_healthy require that service_started does not?","The dependency must define a healthcheck and it must report healthy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-compose-advanced-c2","t":"Healthchecks in Compose","p":"A healthcheck: block in the service overrides or adds to the Dockerfile HEALTHCHECK. Compose uses it for ordering and docker compose up --wait.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-compose-advanced-c3","t":"Profiles","p":"A profile tags a service so it only starts when you ask for that profile. Services without a profile always start.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-compose-advanced-c4","t":"Secrets","p":"A Compose secret mounts a file into the container at /run/secrets/<name>. The value never appears in environment variables or docker inspect.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-compose-advanced-c5","t":"Resource limits, restart policy and hardening","p":"Compose can apply the same limits and security options as docker run, so a development stack mirrors production settings.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-compose-advanced-c6","t":"Compose as a CI test harness","p":"Compose can start the real dependencies of your tests (database, queue, mock services) inside a CI job, run the tests, and always clean up.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests","title":"Registries, tags and digests","c":[{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests-c1","t":"How a registry works","p":"A registry is an HTTP server that stores image manifests and layer blobs, addressed by repository name and by digest. Docker Hub, ECR, GHCR, Harbor and Quay all speak the same OCI Distribution API.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests-c2","t":"Tags are mutable, digests are not","p":"A tag is a label that a push can move to different content. A digest is the SHA-256 of the manifest, so it names exactly one set of bytes.","k":[["Why deploy by digest even when you also keep a version tag?","The digest cannot be re-pointed, so the cluster, an audit and a signature all refer to the same bytes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests-c3","t":"A tagging strategy","p":"Pick a small set of tag types and give each a meaning. Every build gets a unique immutable tag; moving tags are promotion pointers.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests-c4","t":"Amazon ECR","p":"ECR is AWS's private registry. Access is controlled by IAM, and a login token is valid for 12 hours.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests-c5","t":"GitHub Container Registry (GHCR)","p":"GHCR (ghcr.io) stores images next to your code and uses GitHub identities and permissions. In Actions, the built in GITHUBTOKEN can push.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-registries-tags-digests-c6","t":"Pull rate limits, mirrors and private registries","p":"Public registries throttle anonymous pulls. Large cluster scale-out events can hit those limits and leave pods in ImagePullBackOff.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain","title":"Signing images and the supply chain","c":[{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain-c1","t":"Supply chain threats","p":"The supply chain is everything between source code and the running container: dependencies, base image, build system, registry and deploy tooling.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain-c2","t":"Signing with cosign","p":"cosign (Sigstore) signs an image digest and stores the signature as another artifact in the same registry. Anyone can verify it against your identity or key.","k":[["What does keyless signing remove from your operations?","The long lived private key that must be stored, rotated and protected."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain-c3","t":"SBOMs and attestations","p":"An SBOM (software bill of materials) lists every package inside an image. An attestation is a signed statement about an image, such as its SBOM or build provenance.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain-c4","t":"Provenance and SLSA","p":"Provenance is a signed record of how an image was built: which repository, commit, workflow and builder. SLSA is a graded framework (levels 1 to 3 for builds) for how trustworthy that record is.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain-c5","t":"Enforcing verification in Kubernetes","p":"Signing only helps if the cluster refuses unsigned or unknown images. An admission controller checks the signature when a pod is created.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-signing-supply-chain-c6","t":"Base image updates and dependency automation","p":"Most vulnerabilities in a container arrive through the base image and dependencies, so keeping them current is the cheapest control available.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s13-image-hardening","title":"Image hardening","c":[{"id":"s13-image-hardening-c1","t":"Minimal base images","p":"The smaller the image, the fewer binaries an attacker can use and the fewer CVEs a scanner finds. Distroless images contain your app and runtime libraries but no shell or package manager.","k":[["How do you debug a distroless container with no shell?","Use kubectl debug with an ephemeral container, or run the :debug image variant locally."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-image-hardening-c2","t":"Vulnerability scanning with Trivy","p":"A scanner compares the packages in an image against vulnerability databases (CVEs) and reports severity, the installed version and the fixed version.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-image-hardening-c3","t":"Reading scan results","p":"Not every finding needs a fix today. Triage by whether the vulnerable code is reachable, a fix exists and the severity rating.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-image-hardening-c4","t":"Capabilities, seccomp and no-new-privileges","p":"Linux capabilities split root powers into about 40 pieces. A web app needs none of them, so drop them all and add back only what is proven necessary.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-image-hardening-c5","t":"Read-only root filesystem and secrets handling","p":"If the root filesystem is read-only, an attacker cannot drop tools or modify binaries. Writable paths are explicit and small.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-image-hardening-c6","t":"Hardening checklist and CIS benchmark","p":"A checklist turns the scattered hardening ideas into an auditable baseline. The CIS Docker Benchmark and the Kubernetes Pod Security Standards describe the same controls.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s14-oci-standards","title":"OCI standards and image formats","c":[{"id":"s14-oci-standards-c1","t":"The three OCI specifications","p":"The OCI publishes three specifications: the Image spec (what an image is), the Runtime spec (how to run a container from a filesystem bundle) and the Distribution spec (how registries serve images).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-oci-standards-c2","t":"Anatomy of an image on disk","p":"An OCI layout is a folder with an index.json, an oci-layout marker and a blobs/sha256/ directory. Every file in blobs/ is named by the SHA-256 of its contents.","k":[["What is the difference between a layer digest and a diff_id?","The digest is the hash of the compressed blob as stored; the diff_id is the hash of the uncompressed tar, used to identify content independent of compression."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-oci-standards-c3","t":"Media types, compression and multi-platform images","p":"Each blob has a media type that tells tools how to read it. A single tag can point to an index containing one manifest per CPU architecture.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-oci-standards-c4","t":"The runtime bundle and config.json","p":"To run a container, the runtime needs a bundle: a directory with a rootfs/ (the unpacked image layers) and a config.json describing process, mounts, namespaces and limits.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-oci-standards-c5","t":"Building without a daemon","p":"Because images are just files, several tools build or assemble them without Docker: Buildah, Kaniko, BuildKit rootless, ko (Go) and Jib (Java).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-oci-standards-c6","t":"OCI artifacts and referrers","p":"A registry can store more than runnable images. The same manifest format carries Helm charts, SBOMs, signatures and policy bundles as OCI artifacts, linked to an image through the referrers API.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s15-containerd-runc","title":"containerd, runc and the runtime stack","c":[{"id":"s15-containerd-runc-c1","t":"The runtime stack","p":"Starting a container passes through several programs, each with one job: an orchestrator asks a high level runtime (containerd), which asks a shim, which calls a low level runtime (runc), which makes the kernel calls and then exits.","k":[["Why can containerd restart without stopping running containers?","Each container's parent is its own shim process, not the containerd daemon."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-containerd-runc-c2","t":"containerd and its namespaces","p":"containerd stores containers and images in namespaces, which are just logical groups inside containerd. Kubernetes uses k8s.io, Docker uses moby.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-containerd-runc-c3","t":"runc in action","p":"runc is a small command that reads a bundle and starts the container. It does the kernel work then leaves; the shim keeps watching.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-containerd-runc-c4","t":"Alternative runtimes and RuntimeClass","p":"Because the runtime spec is standard, you can replace runc with a runtime that adds a stronger isolation boundary, and choose it per pod.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-containerd-runc-c5","t":"Image pull path and lazy pulling","p":"When a pod starts on a new node the image pull often dominates startup time. containerd downloads the manifest, then layers in parallel, decompresses and unpacks each one into a snapshot.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-containerd-runc-c6","t":"Troubleshooting on a Kubernetes node","p":"When kubectl shows a pod stuck in ContainerCreating or ImagePullBackOff, the cause is often visible only on the node, in kubelet and containerd logs.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s16-debugging-basics","title":"Debugging containers part one","c":[{"id":"s16-debugging-basics-c1","t":"A debugging order","p":"Ask the same questions in the same order: is it running, why did it stop, what did it say, what is its configuration, what can it reach.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-debugging-basics-c2","t":"Exit codes","p":"When a container stops, its exit code says why. Codes above 128 mean the process was killed by signal number code minus 128.","k":[["What does exit code 137 with OOMKilled false suggest?","An external SIGKILL, such as docker kill, a failed liveness probe or the stop timeout expiring, not a memory limit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-debugging-basics-c3","t":"Logs","p":"Containers should write logs to stdout and stderr. The engine captures them and docker logs or kubectl logs reads them.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-debugging-basics-c4","t":"docker inspect and format templates","p":"docker inspect prints everything the engine knows about an object as JSON. Go templates or jq pull out just the part you need.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-debugging-basics-c5","t":"docker exec and one off containers","p":"docker exec starts an extra process inside a running container's namespaces. docker run creates a new container, handy to reproduce a problem with the same image.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-debugging-basics-c6","t":"Networking checks from inside","p":"Most \"service is down\" reports are really DNS, port, route or firewall problems. A short set of probes inside the container tells which.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced","title":"Debugging containers part two","c":[{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced-c1","t":"Sharing namespaces with a toolbox container","p":"You can start a second container that joins the namespaces of the broken one. It brings its own tools but sees the target's network, processes or filesystem.","k":[["Why is --network container:NAME better than installing curl into the target?","The target stays unchanged, so the evidence is intact, and the same image keeps its small attack surface."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced-c2","t":"Ephemeral debug containers in Kubernetes","p":"kubectl debug adds a temporary container to a running pod. It shares the pod's network, can share the target container's process namespace, and is removed when the pod is replaced.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced-c3","t":"nsenter from the host","p":"nsenter runs a command from the host inside any of a process's namespaces. It needs the PID of the container's main process on the host.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced-c4","t":"strace, tcpdump and /proc","p":"When logs are silent, look at system calls and packets. strace shows every syscall the process makes; tcpdump shows the bytes on the wire; /proc shows live state without any tool.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced-c5","t":"Common failure patterns","p":"A short catalogue of symptoms, their usual cause and the quickest confirmation.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-debugging-advanced-c6","t":"Debugging performance and resource problems","p":"Slow containers are usually CPU throttled, memory starved, waiting on disk or limited by the process count. The cgroup files and docker stats show which.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache","title":"BuildKit and CI build caching","c":[{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache-c1","t":"BuildKit","p":"BuildKit is the build engine behind docker build and docker buildx build. It turns a Dockerfile into a dependency graph and runs independent steps in parallel.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache-c2","t":"Why CI builds are slow","p":"A CI runner is usually a fresh virtual machine. It has no layers, no pip or npm cache, and no previous build to compare with, so every step runs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache-c3","t":"Cache backends","p":"--cache-to exports the build cache and --cache-from imports it. The backend decides where the cache lives.","k":[["What does mode=max add compared with mode=min?","It exports every intermediate layer of all stages, not only the final image layers, so multi-stage builds get cache hits."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache-c4","t":"GitHub Actions workflow with cache","p":"The standard pipeline uses docker/setup-buildx-action, logs in, builds with the gha cache and pushes tags generated from metadata.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache-c5","t":"Keeping the context and cache keys small","p":"Every byte in the build context is hashed and possibly uploaded, and any change to a copied file invalidates the layers after it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-buildkit-ci-cache-c6","t":"Parallel jobs, test stages and builders at scale","p":"Treat the Dockerfile as the pipeline: a test stage runs the tests, a lint stage runs linters, and the runtime stage is what you ship. CI builds named targets.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx","title":"Multi-architecture builds with buildx","c":[{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx-c1","t":"Why multi-arch","p":"CPU architectures run different machine code. An amd64 binary cannot run on an arm64 node. A multi-arch image bundles one variant per platform behind a single tag.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx-c2","t":"Three ways to build for another architecture","p":"You can emulate the other CPU, cross compile on your own CPU, or build natively on a machine of each architecture.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx-c3","t":"buildx builders and building the index","p":"docker buildx manages builder instances. The default Docker driver cannot produce multi-platform images; the docker-container driver can.","k":[["Why cannot the default docker driver build multi-platform images?","It builds only for the engine's platform and cannot export an index; the docker-container driver runs a full BuildKit that can."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx-c4","t":"Native builders with a merge step","p":"For the best speed, build each architecture on a matching runner, push each by digest only, then create the index from those digests.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx-c5","t":"Architecture pitfalls","p":"A multi-arch tag proves both images exist, not that both work. The dependencies and base images must also support each architecture.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-multiarch-buildx-c6","t":"Verifying and running multi-arch images","p":"After pushing, confirm the index lists every platform and run a smoke test for each one before relying on the tag.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example","title":"Complete Node and Python services","c":[{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example-c1","t":"Node service files","p":"A small HTTP service with a health endpoint, graceful shutdown and a multi-stage Dockerfile that ends in a non-root runtime image.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example-c2","t":"Node compose file and run","p":"A compose file that builds the image, applies hardening and includes a MongoDB sidecar behind a profile.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example-c3","t":"Python service files","p":"A FastAPI service reading its database password from a secret file, with health endpoint and a multi-stage Dockerfile using a virtualenv.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example-c4","t":"Python compose file with a secret","p":"Compose ties the API to Postgres, passes the password as a mounted file and waits for the database to be healthy.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example-c5","t":"Release checklist and capstone verification","p":"Before an image goes to a registry, check it against a short, scriptable list. Each item maps to a concept covered in this section.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-ci-complete-example-c6","t":"Local development workflow","p":"The same Dockerfile and compose file serve development: a bind mounted source tree with auto reload, a dev stage with test tools, and a fast loop with docker compose watch.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives","title":"Docker Desktop alternatives","c":[{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c1","t":"What you actually need from Docker","p":"Docker is several parts: a CLI, a daemon (dockerd) with an API, a runtime (containerd and runc), a builder (BuildKit) and, on Mac and Windows, a Linux VM.","k":[["Why do containers on a Mac need a VM?","Containers use Linux kernel features (namespaces, cgroups), so a Linux kernel must run in a VM."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c2","t":"Podman","p":"Podman is a daemonless, rootless-friendly container engine with a Docker-compatible CLI. Each container is a child process of the command that started it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c3","t":"Colima and Lima","p":"Colima runs a small Linux VM (through Lima) and installs the Docker or containerd runtime in it. You keep the normal docker CLI.","k":[["What does the Docker CLI use to talk to Colima?","A Docker context that points to a socket forwarded from the Lima VM."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c4","t":"nerdctl and containerd","p":"nerdctl is a Docker-compatible CLI for containerd. It adds features such as lazy pulling, image encryption and rootless support.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c5","t":"Buildah","p":"Buildah builds OCI images without a daemon, using either Dockerfiles or step-by-step shell commands.","k":[["Why prefer daemonless builders in CI?","No Docker socket (root access) needs to be mounted into the job, which reduces the risk of a container escape."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c6","t":"Kaniko and in-cluster builds","p":"Kaniko builds images from a Dockerfile inside a container or Kubernetes Pod without privileged mode or a daemon.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-desktop-alternatives-c7","t":"Choosing a toolchain","p":"Pick by environment: developer laptop, CI, cluster node. They need not be the same tool.","k":[],"o":[["Colima","you want the docker CLI and Compose unchanged","you need a GUI"],["Podman","you want rootless and daemonless","tools need the Docker socket and you cannot map it"],["Docker Desktop","policy allows it and you want the GUI and extensions","licence cost or resource use is an issue"]]}]},{"id":"s22-rootless-userns","title":"Rootless containers and user namespaces","c":[{"id":"s22-rootless-userns-c1","t":"User namespaces","p":"A user namespace gives a process its own view of user and group IDs. UID 0 inside can map to an ordinary UID outside.","k":[["What does the mapping 0 100000 65536 mean?","Inside IDs 0 to 65535 correspond to outside IDs 100000 to 165535."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-rootless-userns-c2","t":"Rootful versus rootless runtimes","p":"Rootless means the container engine itself runs as a normal user. Rootful engines have a root daemon but can still run non-root containers.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-rootless-userns-c3","t":"Limits and workarounds","p":"Rootless mode cannot do everything root can. Knowing the limits avoids surprises.","k":[["Why do bind-mounted files look owned by a strange UID in rootless mode?","The container user maps to a subordinate UID on the host. keep-id or chown mapping aligns it with your user."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-rootless-userns-c4","t":"Dockerfile practices for rootless and non-root","p":"Images should run as a fixed non-root user and avoid needing root for startup, so they work in rootless engines and restricted Kubernetes namespaces.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-rootless-userns-c5","t":"User namespaces in Kubernetes","p":"Kubernetes can run Pods in their own user namespace with hostUsers: false, so root in a container is unprivileged on the node.","k":[["What does hostUsers false change?","The Pod gets its own user namespace, so UID 0 inside maps to a high unprivileged UID on the node."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-rootless-userns-c6","t":"Verifying isolation in practice","p":"After enabling rootless or user namespaces, prove it from the host side. Trust the evidence, not the setting.","k":[["What host-side output proves container root is remapped?","ps shows the sleep process owned by a high, unprivileged UID and uid_map has a non-identity mapping."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive","title":"Container networking deep dive","c":[{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c1","t":"Network namespaces and veth pairs","p":"Each container has its own network namespace with its own interfaces, routes and firewall rules. A veth pair is a virtual cable between two namespaces.","k":[["Which container owns the Pod network namespace in Kubernetes?","The pause (sandbox) container. Application containers join its namespace."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c2","t":"The bridge network and NAT","p":"Docker's default bridge connects containers on a private subnet. iptables NAT rules translate their addresses when they talk outward and forward published ports in.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c3","t":"User-defined networks and DNS","p":"Custom bridge networks add automatic DNS by container name and better isolation than the default bridge.","k":[["What does --internal do to a network?","Containers on it cannot reach outside networks. They can talk only to each other."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c4","t":"Host and none modes","p":"--network host shares the host's network namespace. --network none gives only loopback.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c5","t":"macvlan and ipvlan","p":"macvlan gives each container its own MAC and IP on your physical network, so it looks like a separate machine. ipvlan shares the parent's MAC but gives separate IPs.","k":[["Why can the host not ping its own macvlan containers?","The kernel does not pass traffic between a parent interface and its macvlan children. A shim interface on the host fixes it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c6","t":"Overlay networks and Swarm","p":"An overlay network connects containers across several hosts by wrapping their traffic in VXLAN tunnels.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-networking-deep-dive-c7","t":"Debugging container networks","p":"Debug in layers: interface, address, route, DNS, firewall, then application.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs","title":"Image optimisation labs","c":[{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs-c1","t":"Lab 1, measure first","p":"Record size, layer sizes, build time and vulnerability count for the current image. These numbers are your baseline.","k":[["Why time both a clean build and an incremental rebuild?","Clean build shows CI worst case; incremental shows the developer loop and cache effectiveness."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs-c2","t":"Lab 2, choose a smaller base image","p":"The base image is often most of the size. Compare full, slim, Alpine, distroless and scratch options for your language.","k":[],"o":[["Slim","most services","you need glibc plus unusual tools"],["Alpine","static or pure-language apps","wheels or native modules fail on musl"],["Distroless","production runtime stage","you must shell in without debug tooling"],["scratch","static Go or Rust binaries","the binary needs certificates, timezone data or a user you did not copy"]]},{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs-c3","t":"Lab 3, shrink layers","p":"A file deleted in a later layer still exists in the earlier layer. Clean up in the same instruction that creates the files.","k":[["Why does deleting a file in a later RUN not reduce image size?","Layers are additive. The earlier layer still stores the file, and the later one only hides it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs-c4","t":"Lab 4, speed up builds with cache and ordering","p":"Order instructions from least to most frequently changing, and use BuildKit cache mounts and remote caches.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs-c5","t":"Lab 5, language specific tips","p":"Each language has its own large files and cheap wins.","k":[["Why layer a Spring Boot jar?","Dependencies change rarely and the application classes often, so separate layers keep the large dependency layer cached."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-image-optimisation-labs-c6","t":"Lab 6, enforce a budget in CI","p":"A budget is a limit on image size, build time and high severity CVEs. CI fails when a change exceeds it.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes","title":"Compose to Kubernetes migration","c":[{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes-c1","t":"Mapping Compose to Kubernetes","p":"Each Compose idea has a Kubernetes counterpart, but often several objects instead of one.","k":[["What replaces depends_on in Kubernetes?","Readiness probes, init containers and client retries. Kubernetes starts Pods independently."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes-c2","t":"kompose","p":"kompose converts a Compose file into Kubernetes manifests. It is a starting point, not a finished deployment.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes-c3","t":"What kompose output needs added","p":"Generated manifests are minimal. Production needs explicit decisions in a checklist.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes-c4","t":"Skaffold","p":"Skaffold automates the inner loop: build the image, push or load it, deploy the manifests and stream logs, rebuilding on file changes.","k":[["When is file sync useful?","For interpreted languages, where changed files can be copied into the running container instead of rebuilding the image."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes-c5","t":"Tilt","p":"Tilt is another inner-loop tool with a web dashboard. It is configured with a Tiltfile written in Starlark, a Python-like language.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-compose-to-kubernetes-c6","t":"Migration plan and pitfalls","p":"Move one service at a time, keep Compose for local development, and run both until the Kubernetes path is proven.","k":[["Why keep Compose during migration?","It remains the quickest local environment and gives a baseline to compare behaviour against."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies","title":"Container security case studies and escape techniques","c":[{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c1","t":"The threat model","p":"A container shares the host kernel. Isolation comes from namespaces, cgroups, capabilities, seccomp, LSMs and the runtime. An attacker in a container tries to cross one of these boundaries.","k":[["Why is a container weaker than a VM as a security boundary?","It shares the host kernel, so a kernel or runtime flaw can cross the boundary."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c2","t":"Case 1, privileged containers","p":"--privileged (or privileged: true in Kubernetes) removes most isolation: all capabilities, all devices, no seccomp, no AppArmor.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c3","t":"Case 2, the Docker socket","p":"Mounting /var/run/docker.sock into a container hands that container control of the Docker daemon, which runs as root on the host.","k":[["Why is access to the Docker socket equal to root on the host?","The daemon runs as root and can create containers that mount the host filesystem."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c4","t":"Case 3, dangerous capabilities and host mounts","p":"Individual capabilities such as SYSADMIN, SYSPTRACE, NETADMIN, DACREADSEARCH and SYSMODULE enable specific attacks. Mounts of host paths expose host data.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c5","t":"Case 4, runc and runtime vulnerabilities","p":"The runtime that sets up containers (runc, containerd) is code running as root. Bugs there can let a malicious image or container overwrite host files.","k":[["Why can a configuration policy not stop a runtime vulnerability?","The flaw is in the code that sets up the container, so only patching the runtime or the kernel fixes it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c6","t":"Case 5, kernel exploits and cgroup escapes","p":"Because the kernel is shared, a kernel bug reachable from a container can break out of it. Some classic escapes abuse cgroup v1 release agents when the container is privileged.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c7","t":"Case 6, Kubernetes specific paths","p":"In clusters, attackers also pivot with service account tokens, the kubelet API, the cloud metadata service and permissive RBAC.","k":[["Which Kubernetes permission is almost equivalent to node access if admission is lax?","create on pods, because a Pod can mount host paths, Secrets and use privileged settings unless policy blocks them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c8","t":"Case 7, image and supply chain compromises","p":"Malicious or vulnerable images, typosquatted names, leaked build secrets and compromised base images are a major real-world route into containers.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-container-security-case-studies-c9","t":"Reviewing a configuration like an attacker","p":"A short checklist turns these cases into a repeatable review for any workload.","k":[["Name three host-exposure settings to deny by policy.","privileged, host namespaces (hostPID, hostNetwork, hostIPC), and hostPath or runtime socket mounts."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a","title":"Docker troubleshooting scenarios 1 to 8","c":[{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c1","t":"Scenario 1, container exits immediately","p":"docker run starts a container and it stops within a second. A container lives only as long as its main process.","k":[["What keeps a container running?","Its main process (PID 1). When that process exits, the container stops."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c2","t":"Scenario 2, exit code 137 and OOM","p":"The container dies with exit code 137. That is 128 plus signal 9 (SIGKILL), often from the out-of-memory killer.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c3","t":"Scenario 3, port already allocated or unreachable","p":"docker run -p 8080:80 fails with \"port is already allocated\", or the port is published but nothing answers.","k":[["Why can an app listening on 127.0.0.1 inside a container be unreachable?","Published ports forward to the container's own IP, not its loopback, so the app must listen on 0.0.0.0."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c4","t":"Scenario 4, DNS and name resolution inside containers","p":"Containers cannot resolve external names, or cannot resolve other containers by name.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c5","t":"Scenario 5, permission denied on volumes","p":"The app cannot write to a mounted directory: permission denied, even though it works without the mount.","k":[["Which mount type copies image ownership into a new volume?","Named volumes do on first use when empty. Bind mounts keep the host's ownership."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c6","t":"Scenario 6, disk full and image bloat","p":"Builds or pulls fail with no space left on device, or the Docker data directory grows without bound.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c7","t":"Scenario 7, build fails with cache or context problems","p":"Builds are slow because the context is huge, or they pick up stale layers, or fail with file not found for a COPY.","k":[["Relative to what are COPY source paths resolved?","The build context directory, not the location of the Dockerfile."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-docker-troubleshooting-a-c8","t":"Scenario 8, cannot pull or push images","p":"docker pull or docker push fails with unauthorized, denied, manifest unknown or TLS errors.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b","title":"Docker troubleshooting scenarios 9 to 15","c":[{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c1","t":"Scenario 9, Compose service starts before its dependency is ready","p":"The app container starts, tries the database, fails and exits, though the database container is \"up\".","k":[["What does plain depends_on guarantee?","Only that the dependency container has been started, not that its service is ready."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c2","t":"Scenario 10, container is unhealthy or stuck restarting","p":"docker ps shows (unhealthy) or Restarting. A healthcheck fails, or the restart policy keeps relaunching a crashing process.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c3","t":"Scenario 11, container ignores stop and takes 10 seconds","p":"docker stop waits ten seconds and then kills the container. The main process does not handle SIGTERM.","k":[["Why use exec form for CMD?","The application runs as PID 1 and receives signals directly instead of a shell that does not forward them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c4","t":"Scenario 12, slow file access on macOS bind mounts","p":"Builds and tests are slow when the project directory is bind mounted into a container on macOS.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c5","t":"Scenario 13, wrong architecture: exec format error","p":"A container fails with exec format error or a warning about platform mismatch.","k":[["What is a manifest list?","An image index that points to one image per platform, so each host pulls the matching architecture."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c6","t":"Scenario 14, high CPU or a runaway container","p":"One container takes all CPU or memory on a shared Docker host and other services slow down.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-docker-troubleshooting-b-c7","t":"Scenario 15, the Docker daemon will not start or hangs","p":"docker ps reports Cannot connect to the Docker daemon or hangs. The daemon failed to start or is stuck.","k":[["Name two quick checks when docker cannot connect to the daemon.","Whether the current context or DOCKER_HOST points to the right socket, and whether the docker service is running (systemctl status docker or colima status)."]],"o":[]}]}]};